Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8

Good questions, answers inline:

> 1. How do we handle encoded headers (RFC 2047)?

Yes, both Q-encoding and B-encoding (base64). The std/string
module has what we need for decoding.

> 2. What about nested multipart?

We'll handle it recursively. Each multipart boundary creates a
new parsing context. The Block ADT's Section type maps naturally
to MIME parts.

> 3. Content-Transfer-Encoding: should we handle both base64 and
>    quoted-printable?

Yes, both. Base64 via std/encoding, QP via a simple decoder
(replace =XX with the byte value, handle soft line breaks).

> 4. For MBOX, the "From " line delimiter

Good catch. We'll use the standard "From " prefix detection and
handle ">From " escaping.

I've created 8 challenge test files covering these cases:
  challenge_basic.eml         - plain text
  challenge_multipart_alt.eml - text + HTML alternative
  challenge_multipart_mixed.eml - text + CSV attachment
  challenge_base64_body.eml   - base64-encoded body
  challenge_qp_body.eml       - quoted-printable with Unicode
  challenge_encoded_headers.eml - RFC 2047 Q + B encoding
  challenge_folded_headers.eml  - long headers wrapped across lines
  challenge_mbox.mbox         - 3-message thread

PR incoming after I verify all golden outputs.

Mark

